WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:03.010
LINNEA SHIVELY: Good evening.

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:05.520
My name is Linnea Shively,
and I'm a freshman here

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.970
at Hillsdale College intending
to major in political economy

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:10.470
and minor in journalism.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.170
It's an honor to introduce
our speaker tonight.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:16.590
Christopher F. Rufo is a
Senior Fellow at the Manhattan

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:18.570
Institute and
Founder and Director

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:22.080
of Battlefront, a public
policy research center.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.620
Author of the recent Imprimis
essay, Critical Race Theory--

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.810
What It Is and How
To Fight It, he

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:30.210
is a graduate of
Georgetown University

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.640
and a former Lincoln
Fellow at the Claremont

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.610
Institute for the Study of
Statesmanship and Political

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:37.110
Philosophy.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:40.320
As Executive Director at
the Documentary Foundation,

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.880
he has directed four films for
PBS, including, most recently,

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.430
America Lost,
which explores life

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:51.280
in Youngstown, Ohio, Memphis,
Tennessee, and Stockton,

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:52.240
California.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.080
He is also a contributing
editor of City Journal

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.480
where he covers topics
including critical race

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:01.080
theory, homelessness,
addiction, and crime.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.360
Please join me in
welcoming Mr. Rufo.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.324
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:14.660
CHRISTOPHER RUFO: All right.

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:15.730
How is everyone?

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:16.510
[AUDIENCE MURMURS]

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:17.010
Good.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:19.000
Good to meet you.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.610
I think I see some of my
students from this last week.

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:24.190
I've been teaching a
one-credit journalism class.

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:26.620
All of the students present
will get an A in the class,

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:27.440
so congratulations.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:28.510
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:29.950
Over there, well done.

00:01:32.298 --> 00:01:33.590
Well, let's see what we can do.

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:35.373
Let's get in some
trouble tonight maybe.

00:01:35.373 --> 00:01:37.540
I know that this is being
recorded and livestreamed,

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:40.750
so I'm hoping to say something
interesting and noteworthy

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:43.180
in the next 30 minutes or so.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:47.660
The topic tonight is Laying
Siege to the Institutions.

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:50.560
And so the question is,
what does that mean?

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.030
But before we can
answer that question,

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:55.720
we have to know the context
in which that question must

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:57.310
be asked.

00:01:57.310 --> 00:02:00.580
And so I'd like us to take
a step back to the period

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:03.010
that none of you remember--
you're all far too young--

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:04.500
the period of 1968.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.388
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:11.170
If you look at
1968, it was really

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:16.090
a paradoxical and
contradictory turning point

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:18.220
in American history.

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:22.390
You had, then, all of the
left-wing ideologies that

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:24.160
seemed so dominant
in our institutions

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:30.520
today being born in the
months in and around 1968.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:33.530
You had the birth of
identity politics.

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:37.630
You had the idea that the United
States could be overthrown

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:40.090
through cultural revolution.

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:44.800
And then you had the very
beginnings of militant street

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.790
activists that had sought
to, what they believed,

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:51.280
use urban guerrilla
warfare to achieve

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:53.900
their political objectives.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:56.980
And I think many of
the stories that I

00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:59.020
work on, many of the
phenomena that we see,

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:02.020
many of the public policies that
some of our friends on the far

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:05.470
left in places like Seattle,
or San Francisco, or New

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:09.250
York, or Chicago are
proposing, if you actually

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:11.110
look back and look
at the literature,

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:16.180
look at the manifestos, look at
the publications of that era,

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:18.490
you see a remarkable similarity.

00:03:18.490 --> 00:03:22.690
It's almost as if we're living
in permanent repeat of some

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:24.700
of those ideas.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:26.330
The only difference,
unfortunately,

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:29.410
is that, in 1968, it was
also the year that was

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:31.720
the election of Richard Nixon--

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:35.560
not exactly a
fire-breathing leftist.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:41.530
And so you saw this left wing
movement burn out over time.

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:45.670
By the mid-1970s, the groups
like the Black Panther Party,

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:48.790
the Black Liberation Army,
the Weather Underground,

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:51.040
or their counterparts
in Europe--

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:54.040
in Germany, and Italy,
and other countries--

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:56.440
that had sought to
really capitalize

00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:00.970
on what they saw as a world
historical change in 1968

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:04.390
had met really a
sad and divided end.

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:07.360
They were infiltrated
by law enforcement.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:09.640
Their campaign for
urban guerrilla warfare

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:12.610
generated discontent,
especially in the communities

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:15.013
that they claimed to represent.

00:04:15.013 --> 00:04:16.680
And so, by the end
of it, there was just

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:20.610
a few dozen angry,
disillusioned, demoralized

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:21.870
radicals.

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:26.500
The FBI had swept
up many of them.

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:28.840
And what you sense
in the literature

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:30.460
and the history of the time--

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:33.520
and you can read it in all
of the journalistic work,

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:37.420
the letters, the essays,
some of the academic work,

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:41.710
is that they realized that
this moment had been lost.

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:45.310
They looked at it as the
victory of counter-revolutionary

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:47.890
forces, embodied most
prominently in the United

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:49.690
States by Richard Nixon.

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:51.730
But really, at the
heart of it, the lesson

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:55.480
that they learned at the time
was that the working class

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:56.950
in the West--

00:04:56.950 --> 00:05:01.990
in Western Europe and especially
in the United States--

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:05.230
could not be roused
to revolution.

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:08.290
That class-based
idea that you could

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:11.440
have the suppressed workers
or the oppressed workers

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:14.830
rise up against their bosses
in the factories in places

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:19.270
like Detroit,
Michigan was doomed.

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:22.000
And, in fact,
working-class Americans

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:26.260
were not only non-revolutionary,
they were anti-revolutionary.

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:29.710
They were actually satisfied
with a rapidly rising standard

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:34.210
of living, with benefits and
privileges that their parents

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:37.450
and grandparents could not
have even imagined, perhaps,

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:41.800
when they were immigrating from
Europe in the decades prior.

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:45.810
And so this immense frustration,
by the time you hit the 1970s,

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:50.320
is really evident at all
of the work of the period.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:52.980
And so they settled on a
strategy of the long march

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:55.350
through the institutions.

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:57.780
They realized that they
couldn't set off bombs

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:01.560
in the US Capitol, as they tried
to do, or assassinate police

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:03.900
officers, shooting them in
the back of the head at point

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:06.000
blank range in New
York City, as they

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.730
had done a number of times.

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:12.450
But they actually formulated
a new, more sophisticated,

00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:15.810
and, frankly, very successful
strategy of abandoning

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:18.300
hope that the
lumpen proletariat--

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:22.110
so the outcasts of society,
people who are truly

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:26.100
the downtrodden and forgotten
remnants of the American class

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:28.830
system, they really abandoned
hope that they could

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:30.362
be part of this revolution.

00:06:30.362 --> 00:06:32.070
They said, we're going
to go a revolution

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:35.520
of the intelligentsia,
a revolution of elites,

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:39.365
by seizing control of not
of the means of production--

00:06:39.365 --> 00:06:41.490
you'd be hard pressed to
get some academics running

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:44.520
a factory line in Detroit--

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:49.000
but through the means of culture
and knowledge production.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.000
And so what you see is
these washed-up radicals,

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:56.710
in the '60s, '70s, early-'80s,
migrating into academia,

00:06:56.710 --> 00:07:03.640
and then bringing their ideas,
for example, in the 1970s,

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:06.430
out of the radical manifestos,
the Marxist-Leninist

00:07:06.430 --> 00:07:11.095
revolutionary manifestos, and
into formalized academic work.

00:07:11.095 --> 00:07:12.970
What I've done-- we're
going to fast forward,

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:14.570
we have a limited
amount of time--

00:07:14.570 --> 00:07:16.660
but what I've done
in the last two years

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:20.140
is report on the state of
our institutions today.

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:22.120
I've looked at the
federal government.

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:24.370
I've looked at the universities.

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:27.280
I've looked at
Fortune 100 companies.

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:30.170
And I've looked at K
through 12 schools.

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:31.920
And what I found is
something that I think

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:35.850
is shocking to most Americans.

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:38.670
You're taking the core
concepts that were developed

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:41.880
in that period after 1968.

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:48.000
They've been sanitized, adapted,
repackaged, and repurposed,

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:50.490
and then injected
into American life

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:54.130
at the lateral, bureaucratic,
and institutional level.

00:07:54.130 --> 00:07:58.680
So you have an idea that seems
somewhat frivolous, maybe

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:02.190
somewhat ridiculous, like
white privilege, that's

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:04.200
being taught, in many
stories that I've

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:08.820
reported on, to kids as young as
seven, eight, nine years old--

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:12.900
that children in a classroom
can be separated into oppressor

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:15.210
and oppressed based on
identity categories,

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:21.120
based on skin color, and then
deemed oppressor and oppressed.

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:23.730
And then the kids in the
public classroom, let's say,

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:26.430
in Cupertino, California-- they
recently did this for third

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:28.020
graders--

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:30.840
should atone for
their white privilege.

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:32.280
Some of it seems a little goofy.

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:36.880
It seems a little
bit ridiculous.

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:38.919
But the origins of
white privilege theory,

00:08:38.919 --> 00:08:40.630
again, were from 1968.

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:42.460
It was a
Marxist-Leninist theory.

00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:44.830
The idea is that you'd
have to break down

00:08:44.830 --> 00:08:48.640
working-class notions of white
privilege in order for them

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:52.570
to join the Marxist revolution.

00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:54.850
And so the purpose and
the origins of a lot

00:08:54.850 --> 00:08:57.400
of these ideas, as
you go down the line,

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:00.850
all emerges from that
point and now has

00:09:00.850 --> 00:09:06.190
been transferred through
academic literature,

00:09:06.190 --> 00:09:09.430
through K-through-12
pedagogy, through masters

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:13.840
in education programs, through
K-through-12 curriculum

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:17.890
modules, through HR programs--
human resources departments--

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:20.060
at corporations.

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:21.820
And I think what we
find ourselves in

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:27.010
is a maze of postmodern language
that is the other element that

00:09:27.010 --> 00:09:31.280
is grafted onto the
revolutionary rhetoric of 60,

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:33.680
70 years ago now.

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:36.500
And it's a language
of obfuscation,

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:38.900
it's a language of
euphemism, and it's

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:41.450
a language of demoralization.

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:43.760
And so as many of you have
been following, for example,

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:46.910
the fight against critical
race theory, one of the fights

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:49.340
that has been really
going on for a year,

00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:52.430
almost to the
point of absurdity,

00:09:52.430 --> 00:09:55.190
is this euphemistic
game of, well,

00:09:55.190 --> 00:09:56.690
what is critical race theory?

00:09:56.690 --> 00:09:58.520
That's not critical race theory.

00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:01.100
Critical race theory is an
obscure legal theory only found

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:03.530
in upper-division
law school classes.

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:06.230
Oh, well, your K through
12 elementary curriculum

00:10:06.230 --> 00:10:08.510
says critical race
theory on the cover?

00:10:08.510 --> 00:10:11.180
It's using all the principles
of critical race theory?

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:12.320
That couldn't be.

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:13.970
It couldn't exist.

00:10:13.970 --> 00:10:17.250
That's diversity,
equity, and inclusion.

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:22.460
And so you get these ideas that
are wrapped in language that

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:27.500
is meant to obscure
or meant to be

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:30.560
impenetrable or un-opposable.

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:34.610
I think none of us, very
few people, certainly,

00:10:34.610 --> 00:10:36.770
in the United States--
and to a lesser extent,

00:10:36.770 --> 00:10:38.480
but also in the world--

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:41.570
would oppose diversity in
the true sense of the word,

00:10:41.570 --> 00:10:44.660
meaning having a
variety of experiences,

00:10:44.660 --> 00:10:47.210
interacting with a
different variety of people.

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:49.730
This is why Americans travel.

00:10:49.730 --> 00:10:51.200
This is why you go overseas.

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:53.750
You want to get a
diversity of perspective,

00:10:53.750 --> 00:10:57.320
and culture, opinion, history.

00:10:57.320 --> 00:11:00.050
That's not what it means
in practice, though.

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:06.080
The next term, equity,
it appeals to this idea

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:09.350
that's a homonym with equality.

00:11:09.350 --> 00:11:13.190
And yet the significance of
it is the exact opposite.

00:11:13.190 --> 00:11:15.530
Equality is the idea
that each individual

00:11:15.530 --> 00:11:17.660
should be protected
under the law

00:11:17.660 --> 00:11:25.670
regardless of characteristics
that might be separating them

00:11:25.670 --> 00:11:28.050
into different groups
or categories, this idea

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:30.050
that no matter who you
are, where you come from,

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:32.690
you'll be treated
equally under the law.

00:11:32.690 --> 00:11:34.640
Equity takes the opposite view.

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:37.550
It would replace the
system of individual rights

00:11:37.550 --> 00:11:41.820
with the system of group
identity-based rights, in that

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:46.440
your merit, or your
worth, or your position,

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:50.640
or the material resources
that are divided and then

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:55.290
distributed, should be based not
on your individual potential,

00:11:55.290 --> 00:11:57.840
or character, or work,
or accomplishment,

00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:02.790
but based on your
group identity that

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:06.330
takes no account of your
individual characteristics

00:12:06.330 --> 00:12:09.780
or excellence.

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:13.080
And so what I've tried
to do in my reporting

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:19.950
is to surface all of these
very dense and very euphemistic

00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:22.770
phrases and terms
that you're probably

00:12:22.770 --> 00:12:24.810
seeing popping up everywhere.

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:26.430
You're probably
seeing it at work.

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:29.790
You might be seeing it in
your kids' K-through-12 school

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:30.870
curriculum.

00:12:30.870 --> 00:12:33.870
You're certainly seeing it
in the political sphere.

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:38.190
And our federal government is
absolutely saturated with it.

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:39.950
And it's trying to
make heads or tails.

00:12:39.950 --> 00:12:42.380
It's trying to make
sense of this language.

00:12:42.380 --> 00:12:46.670
It's trying to get to the
heart of what these words mean,

00:12:46.670 --> 00:12:49.580
because I think that
only then you can truly

00:12:49.580 --> 00:12:51.380
have a sense of how
you can defeat it,

00:12:51.380 --> 00:12:53.660
how you can fight back.

00:12:53.660 --> 00:12:58.490
Because what I've seen, over
and over, in my reporting,

00:12:58.490 --> 00:13:00.590
looking at original
source documents,

00:13:00.590 --> 00:13:04.100
is that every elite
institution in the country that

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:09.410
has dominance over knowledge,
dominance over culture,

00:13:09.410 --> 00:13:13.040
dominance over even, in many
cases, material production,

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:16.310
has converged on a
unitary ideology.

00:13:16.310 --> 00:13:19.010
It's really a remarkable thing.

00:13:19.010 --> 00:13:23.180
The HR training program
at Walmart, for example,

00:13:23.180 --> 00:13:28.070
is the same as the
fifth-grade education

00:13:28.070 --> 00:13:31.550
module in Buffalo
public schools,

00:13:31.550 --> 00:13:34.700
is the same as the diversity
and inclusion training

00:13:34.700 --> 00:13:39.890
program at the Department of
the Treasury in Washington, DC.

00:13:39.890 --> 00:13:41.840
All three examples
from my reporting.

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:46.730
And it's a very basic list of
concepts derived, in this case,

00:13:46.730 --> 00:13:48.260
from critical race
theory, that says

00:13:48.260 --> 00:13:50.810
the United States is
fundamentally an irredeemably

00:13:50.810 --> 00:13:53.100
a racist country.

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:58.510
The country, today, is saturated
through with systemic racism.

00:13:58.510 --> 00:14:02.500
In the racial categorization of
citizens in the here and now,

00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:05.290
it can be divided on
the axis of oppressor

00:14:05.290 --> 00:14:07.360
and oppressed based on race.

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:11.440
And people who are deemed
white or, in some cases,

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:16.360
white enough-- like
Asian-Americans, strangely--

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:19.390
you have to atone
for your privilege,

00:14:19.390 --> 00:14:22.900
you have to bankrupt
your privilege,

00:14:22.900 --> 00:14:26.350
and then if you want to be
re-accepted back into society

00:14:26.350 --> 00:14:29.200
after that process,
you have to submit

00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:33.880
to the political ideology
that regards not freedom

00:14:33.880 --> 00:14:37.720
and equality, not the
Constitution, not the principle

00:14:37.720 --> 00:14:39.730
of equal protection,
not even the system

00:14:39.730 --> 00:14:43.120
of individual rights,
but moving to a system

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:46.330
of collective
group-based rights,

00:14:46.330 --> 00:14:49.930
moving to a system that is
explicitly anti-capitalist.

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:51.430
You have organizations,
for example,

00:14:51.430 --> 00:14:54.130
like American Express
preaching that capitalism

00:14:54.130 --> 00:14:56.800
is fundamentally racist--

00:14:56.800 --> 00:14:59.830
American Express
teaching that capitalism

00:14:59.830 --> 00:15:06.580
is fundamentally racist
and so on and so forth.

00:15:06.580 --> 00:15:09.180
And one of the great
curiosities for me

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:11.580
was, how is this even possible?

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:17.430
I'm sorting through documents
from 5,000 different sources,

00:15:17.430 --> 00:15:20.070
all over the country, from
every kind of institution,

00:15:20.070 --> 00:15:22.650
from all walks of life.

00:15:22.650 --> 00:15:25.250
How is it possible that
it's been distilled down

00:15:25.250 --> 00:15:29.390
to these key, say,
seven to 10 ideas that

00:15:29.390 --> 00:15:32.390
are then implanted
into the organizations

00:15:32.390 --> 00:15:34.410
through the bureaucracy?

00:15:34.410 --> 00:15:36.680
So it's either the
DEI bureaucracy--

00:15:36.680 --> 00:15:39.210
that's kind of the
most common one now--

00:15:39.210 --> 00:15:41.130
human resources is
another one, there's

00:15:41.130 --> 00:15:42.660
all sorts of
different euphemisms

00:15:42.660 --> 00:15:47.250
that get constantly
created and recreated.

00:15:47.250 --> 00:15:49.290
But the ideology is the same.

00:15:49.290 --> 00:15:52.050
And the ideology is
really indistinguishable,

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:54.840
at its essence, from a
lot of those materials

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:58.730
that we saw in 1968.

00:15:58.730 --> 00:16:03.140
And so the question is, what
can we do to fight back?

00:16:03.140 --> 00:16:04.850
I'm going to talk
about tactics and then

00:16:04.850 --> 00:16:06.860
I'm going to talk
about strategy.

00:16:06.860 --> 00:16:10.370
As far as tactics, I've
been tasked and asked

00:16:10.370 --> 00:16:12.260
to talk a little
bit about a story

00:16:12.260 --> 00:16:15.620
that I broke last week while
I was teaching the journalism

00:16:15.620 --> 00:16:18.650
students here at Hillsdale
College about a company

00:16:18.650 --> 00:16:22.140
called Walt Disney.

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:25.070
So any news watchers
in the room?

00:16:25.070 --> 00:16:26.330
Yeah, OK.

00:16:26.330 --> 00:16:29.330
So you've probably heard
about this scandal,

00:16:29.330 --> 00:16:32.690
really, with the
Walt Disney Company.

00:16:32.690 --> 00:16:34.910
I've actually been reporting
on Walt Disney for more

00:16:34.910 --> 00:16:36.410
than a year, off and on.

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:38.780
I have some good sources
inside the company.

00:16:38.780 --> 00:16:41.720
I broke a story last year
that Walt Disney Company

00:16:41.720 --> 00:16:45.770
was forcing employees to engage
in a critical race theory

00:16:45.770 --> 00:16:48.440
training program which
denounced the United

00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:52.310
States as a fundamentally
racist country,

00:16:52.310 --> 00:16:55.220
asked its white employees to
complete a white privilege

00:16:55.220 --> 00:17:00.800
checklist, and then
advocated for all

00:17:00.800 --> 00:17:03.440
of the fashionable causes.

00:17:03.440 --> 00:17:05.780
I believe they had
exercises in there

00:17:05.780 --> 00:17:10.460
to decolonize your bookshelf,
whatever that means.

00:17:10.460 --> 00:17:13.910
And any kind of these
pedagogical techniques or kind

00:17:13.910 --> 00:17:18.260
of fashionable
self-reflection exercises

00:17:18.260 --> 00:17:20.210
that you see in all
of these corporations,

00:17:20.210 --> 00:17:22.819
Disney was doing it.

00:17:22.819 --> 00:17:26.720
After I broke the story,
Disney's first attempt

00:17:26.720 --> 00:17:28.280
was to deflect.

00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:30.620
They sent out this amazing
press release, actually.

00:17:30.620 --> 00:17:33.860
They said-- because people
were criticizing-- they said,

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:34.940
this is racist.

00:17:34.940 --> 00:17:37.880
You're dividing your employees
along the basis of race.

00:17:37.880 --> 00:17:42.500
They've created
racially-segregated employee

00:17:42.500 --> 00:17:44.118
activist groups.

00:17:44.118 --> 00:17:45.410
They're denouncing the country.

00:17:45.410 --> 00:17:47.493
They're denouncing a large
part of their employees

00:17:47.493 --> 00:17:49.832
and their customers on
the basis of skin color.

00:17:49.832 --> 00:17:51.290
And Disney said in
a press release,

00:17:51.290 --> 00:17:53.330
no, no, no, we
can't be racist, we

00:17:53.330 --> 00:17:56.450
made the movie Black Panther.

00:17:56.450 --> 00:17:59.630
Which is a kind of bizarre
corporate variation

00:17:59.630 --> 00:18:02.790
on the old joke about,
I can't be racist,

00:18:02.790 --> 00:18:04.430
I have Black friends.

00:18:04.430 --> 00:18:09.050
It was really a kind
of ridiculous moment

00:18:09.050 --> 00:18:10.790
where they were
caught, you could see,

00:18:10.790 --> 00:18:14.180
between the lines of the press
release, totally off-guard.

00:18:14.180 --> 00:18:16.880
Because in the
elite milieu, this

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:19.580
is something that is
not even questioned.

00:18:19.580 --> 00:18:22.520
In fact, the Disney employees
who leaked me these documents

00:18:22.520 --> 00:18:25.160
and then the video said,
you cannot question them.

00:18:25.160 --> 00:18:26.840
You can't say a word.

00:18:26.840 --> 00:18:27.920
You have to be silent.

00:18:27.920 --> 00:18:29.720
This is the new orthodoxy.

00:18:29.720 --> 00:18:32.240
This is the orthodoxy in
every elite institution.

00:18:32.240 --> 00:18:36.000
If you want to keep your
job, you have to shut up.

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:38.970
Disney eventually deleted
their entire program

00:18:38.970 --> 00:18:41.070
from their internal website--

00:18:41.070 --> 00:18:42.300
so a big victory for me.

00:18:42.300 --> 00:18:45.120
That's always fun.

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:48.090
But, of course, these things
get deleted temporarily,

00:18:48.090 --> 00:18:49.410
but they always resurface.

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:53.395
Because it's not that it
was well-intentioned people,

00:18:53.395 --> 00:18:54.770
that they made a
mistake, or they

00:18:54.770 --> 00:18:57.500
had a document in their server
or part of their training

00:18:57.500 --> 00:18:59.840
program that they
didn't agree with.

00:18:59.840 --> 00:19:03.350
These things are baked
into the bureaucracy.

00:19:03.350 --> 00:19:09.110
And so last week, I was
sent a full video, an hour

00:19:09.110 --> 00:19:14.990
and 40 minutes, approximately,
of Disney's internal meetings

00:19:14.990 --> 00:19:17.630
regarding its very
political and very public

00:19:17.630 --> 00:19:19.820
fight with the
governor of Florida,

00:19:19.820 --> 00:19:22.760
a man named Ron DeSantis,
who signed a bill--

00:19:22.760 --> 00:19:24.680
I think a very simple bill--

00:19:24.680 --> 00:19:28.810
that simply said you
have to keep education

00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:31.900
on gender ideology,
sexual orientation,

00:19:31.900 --> 00:19:35.080
and sexuality out
of the classroom

00:19:35.080 --> 00:19:37.900
between the ages of
kindergarten and third grade.

00:19:40.700 --> 00:19:42.440
This seems fairly commonsense.

00:19:42.440 --> 00:19:45.320
I think it has, depending on
the polling question, somewhere

00:19:45.320 --> 00:19:50.410
between 60% and 80% support,
an overwhelming number

00:19:50.410 --> 00:19:51.700
of Americans.

00:19:51.700 --> 00:19:53.860
But the activist
groups within Disney

00:19:53.860 --> 00:19:57.460
were furious,
because they believe

00:19:57.460 --> 00:20:00.220
that Disney, as the world's
largest entertainment

00:20:00.220 --> 00:20:03.310
corporation, certainly
the largest entertainment

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:07.570
corporation in human history
regarding kids' entertainment,

00:20:07.570 --> 00:20:11.020
they believe that they were
the moral voice on children

00:20:11.020 --> 00:20:15.950
and sexuality and that they
wanted to not only oppose

00:20:15.950 --> 00:20:20.230
the legislation, but use all of
the levers of corporate power

00:20:20.230 --> 00:20:22.840
to fundamentally reshape
the narrative about kids

00:20:22.840 --> 00:20:24.870
and sexuality.

00:20:24.870 --> 00:20:30.360
And so I released a series
of videos last week that had,

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:34.250
for example, an executive
producer saying that she was

00:20:34.250 --> 00:20:37.700
deliberately, with the
support of her colleagues,

00:20:37.700 --> 00:20:39.230
inserting what she
called a, quote,

00:20:39.230 --> 00:20:42.500
"not-so-secret gay agenda"
into kids programming,

00:20:42.500 --> 00:20:46.160
targeting kids as
young as two years old.

00:20:46.160 --> 00:20:49.130
Another Disney employee,
a production coordinator,

00:20:49.130 --> 00:20:54.050
said that he had created a
tracking program to make sure

00:20:54.050 --> 00:20:59.780
that the company was including
enough transgender, non-binary,

00:20:59.780 --> 00:21:01.400
and asexual characters.

00:21:01.400 --> 00:21:05.270
I think I have
those terms right.

00:21:05.270 --> 00:21:07.670
And this whole
series of statements,

00:21:07.670 --> 00:21:11.330
which you might imagine
happening at, maybe,

00:21:11.330 --> 00:21:15.380
the Women's Studies
department at Vassar College

00:21:15.380 --> 00:21:18.710
I think rightly
concerned a lot of people

00:21:18.710 --> 00:21:21.920
when it was high-level
executives saying,

00:21:21.920 --> 00:21:26.190
we are going to
fundamentally change

00:21:26.190 --> 00:21:28.410
the relationship between
kids and sexuality

00:21:28.410 --> 00:21:33.660
in the United States by
engineering a narrative based

00:21:33.660 --> 00:21:35.070
on gender ideology.

00:21:35.070 --> 00:21:37.110
We're going to do
so really secretly--

00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:38.985
they didn't make an
announcement about this--

00:21:38.985 --> 00:21:40.485
while, at the same
time, we're going

00:21:40.485 --> 00:21:42.600
to be publicly opposing
any legislation that

00:21:42.600 --> 00:21:46.920
would provide a restriction
on this in public schools.

00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:53.250
And so this was, in short, I
think, a scandal for Disney.

00:21:53.250 --> 00:21:55.127
They were very tough.

00:21:55.127 --> 00:21:56.460
They were kind of chest-beating.

00:21:56.460 --> 00:22:01.620
Executives had gone out, prior
to this story, saying what they

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:04.320
were going to do, talking
trash to the governor,

00:22:04.320 --> 00:22:08.040
committing to overthrowing
this bill and overturning it.

00:22:08.040 --> 00:22:09.810
And then after they
were really pummeled--

00:22:09.810 --> 00:22:13.870
I think Fox News mentioned this
story 300 times in 24 hours

00:22:13.870 --> 00:22:16.540
at one point last week--

00:22:16.540 --> 00:22:18.880
the New York Times
wrote about it.

00:22:18.880 --> 00:22:20.950
They said, this was all
fine until Chris Rufo

00:22:20.950 --> 00:22:22.780
got a hold of it.

00:22:22.780 --> 00:22:26.330
Tried to blame me for it.

00:22:26.330 --> 00:22:28.280
And then the company
went fairly silent.

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:30.780
I think it probably also has
something to do with those poll

00:22:30.780 --> 00:22:35.780
numbers we talked about, where
a huge majority of not just

00:22:35.780 --> 00:22:39.930
Florida voters, but American
voters, American adults, said,

00:22:39.930 --> 00:22:44.260
hey, wait a minute,
this is too much.

00:22:44.260 --> 00:22:47.340
We don't want to have
gender ideology being taken

00:22:47.340 --> 00:22:52.260
from the university and being
forced on kindergarteners

00:22:52.260 --> 00:22:54.570
behind the backs of parents
without informing them

00:22:54.570 --> 00:22:56.813
about what's going on.

00:22:56.813 --> 00:22:58.730
And this is something
we've seen over and over

00:22:58.730 --> 00:23:03.390
and over, whether it's critical
race theory or gender ideology.

00:23:03.390 --> 00:23:06.480
And so I think we've
struck quite a blow.

00:23:06.480 --> 00:23:09.450
Disney was trending on
Twitter-- which is, I think,

00:23:09.450 --> 00:23:11.250
kind of a distorted
approximation

00:23:11.250 --> 00:23:13.860
for what's in the
news, in some ways--

00:23:13.860 --> 00:23:16.390
for an entire week.

00:23:16.390 --> 00:23:18.810
You had conservatives
boycotting, canceling

00:23:18.810 --> 00:23:19.980
Disney subscriptions.

00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:22.800
Anyone cancel a Disney
subscription in the last week?

00:23:22.800 --> 00:23:23.880
OK, good.

00:23:23.880 --> 00:23:27.983
That's at least $500
a month or whatever.

00:23:27.983 --> 00:23:29.400
You had a lot of
people canceling,

00:23:29.400 --> 00:23:32.370
and I think you had a lot
of Americans really raising

00:23:32.370 --> 00:23:33.570
questions--

00:23:33.570 --> 00:23:35.760
what's happening with
our institutions?

00:23:35.760 --> 00:23:38.830
What's happening with kids?

00:23:38.830 --> 00:23:44.100
I also discovered that,
playing a bit of hardball,

00:23:44.100 --> 00:23:48.060
when a company enters
the political debate

00:23:48.060 --> 00:23:50.823
on the issues of
children and sexuality,

00:23:50.823 --> 00:23:52.740
my argument was that,
well, we should actually

00:23:52.740 --> 00:23:57.020
examine their record on
children and sexuality.

00:23:57.020 --> 00:23:59.840
And to my somewhat
surprise, I found out

00:23:59.840 --> 00:24:05.120
that the Walt Disney Company
has had a notorious pattern,

00:24:05.120 --> 00:24:08.030
at least once a year
for the last decade,

00:24:08.030 --> 00:24:12.820
and then before that as
well, of having employees

00:24:12.820 --> 00:24:16.310
arrested for child sex crimes--

00:24:16.310 --> 00:24:19.520
child pornography, child
exploitation, child rape,

00:24:19.520 --> 00:24:20.928
et cetera.

00:24:20.928 --> 00:24:22.970
And not only that-- that's
obviously bad enough--

00:24:22.970 --> 00:24:24.470
it appears from the
coverage that it

00:24:24.470 --> 00:24:26.600
is a disproportionate number.

00:24:26.600 --> 00:24:30.710
The Polk County Sheriff, who's
adjacent to Disney's operations

00:24:30.710 --> 00:24:33.470
in Orlando, Florida, it
seems like every time

00:24:33.470 --> 00:24:37.910
he does a sex sting, he nabs at
least a few Disney employees,

00:24:37.910 --> 00:24:39.860
disproportionate,
I would imagine,

00:24:39.860 --> 00:24:43.130
based on the limited
evidence, to their rate

00:24:43.130 --> 00:24:46.370
in the labor force.

00:24:46.370 --> 00:24:48.650
And so my point is that,
when a company is claiming

00:24:48.650 --> 00:24:50.692
the moral high ground on
these issues of children

00:24:50.692 --> 00:24:53.720
and sexuality, we should
look at their record.

00:24:53.720 --> 00:24:55.940
And of course, they're not
responsible for everything

00:24:55.940 --> 00:24:57.650
their employees do
on their own time.

00:24:57.650 --> 00:24:59.720
But I also found a
few stories as well.

00:25:03.410 --> 00:25:07.160
Disney authorities allegedly
told their cruise ship security

00:25:07.160 --> 00:25:10.970
guard to keep her mouth
shut when she discovered

00:25:10.970 --> 00:25:13.250
that one of the Disney
employees was molesting

00:25:13.250 --> 00:25:16.550
a young boy on the
cruise ship, which

00:25:16.550 --> 00:25:18.740
allowed him to evade arrest.

00:25:18.740 --> 00:25:21.480
And then Disney flew
him back home to India

00:25:21.480 --> 00:25:24.710
so he couldn't stand
to be accountable.

00:25:24.710 --> 00:25:28.430
And also, Disney's cruise
ship lobbyist association,

00:25:28.430 --> 00:25:30.530
the trade association,
of which Disney

00:25:30.530 --> 00:25:34.610
is a member, had advocated
against, had opposed,

00:25:34.610 --> 00:25:36.800
and then significantly
watered down

00:25:36.800 --> 00:25:38.870
legislation that
would have required

00:25:38.870 --> 00:25:40.940
Disney and other
cruise lines to report

00:25:40.940 --> 00:25:44.310
sexual abuse on its cruise
ships in a timely and stringent

00:25:44.310 --> 00:25:44.810
manner.

00:25:47.800 --> 00:25:52.800
And so I think the lesson of
this is that the institutions

00:25:52.800 --> 00:25:56.790
in the United States that have
adopted these ideologies cannot

00:25:56.790 --> 00:25:58.350
be appeased.

00:25:58.350 --> 00:26:01.320
They can't be easily
shifted from within.

00:26:01.320 --> 00:26:04.140
They've been captured at
the bureaucratic level.

00:26:04.140 --> 00:26:06.360
And Disney has the
perfect case study.

00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:08.850
Disney executives have
delegated moral authority

00:26:08.850 --> 00:26:11.040
within the company and
political authority

00:26:11.040 --> 00:26:14.890
within the company to internal
activist organizations,

00:26:14.890 --> 00:26:18.360
whether they're in the race
and sex-segregated identity

00:26:18.360 --> 00:26:22.575
caucuses or in the human
resources departments.

00:26:22.575 --> 00:26:23.950
And so what it's
going to require

00:26:23.950 --> 00:26:26.410
is not a long march
through the institutions.

00:26:26.410 --> 00:26:30.010
I think most conservatives don't
have the ambition of, one day,

00:26:30.010 --> 00:26:33.910
being a diversity and equity
director at a public school

00:26:33.910 --> 00:26:40.100
district, whatever
that might look like.

00:26:40.100 --> 00:26:43.480
So the strategy is not to say,
well, let's march back in,

00:26:43.480 --> 00:26:45.310
let's have a kind
of counter-march,

00:26:45.310 --> 00:26:47.300
I think the strategy
is quite different.

00:26:47.300 --> 00:26:50.150
It's an actual siege strategy.

00:26:50.150 --> 00:26:52.040
It's from the outside.

00:26:52.040 --> 00:26:56.875
And it's taking on our
superior public support.

00:26:56.875 --> 00:26:59.000
Because whether it's critical
race theory or gender

00:26:59.000 --> 00:27:01.460
ideology in schools,
for example,

00:27:01.460 --> 00:27:04.460
we have about a 2-to-1
margin, the public

00:27:04.460 --> 00:27:07.340
supporting our position
and opposing the position

00:27:07.340 --> 00:27:09.600
of the institutions.

00:27:09.600 --> 00:27:11.990
These institutions don't
choose these ideologies

00:27:11.990 --> 00:27:13.550
democratically.

00:27:13.550 --> 00:27:14.930
You didn't vote for it.

00:27:14.930 --> 00:27:16.670
I didn't vote for it.

00:27:16.670 --> 00:27:20.180
They're imposed
anti-democratically by fiat

00:27:20.180 --> 00:27:24.453
through bureaucratic,
not democratic, rule.

00:27:24.453 --> 00:27:25.870
And so what we're
seeing, I think,

00:27:25.870 --> 00:27:31.960
as the first step is a
narrative and symbolic war

00:27:31.960 --> 00:27:35.780
against companies like
Disney, for one example.

00:27:35.780 --> 00:27:37.360
You have to be very aggressive.

00:27:37.360 --> 00:27:40.360
You have to fight on
terms that you define.

00:27:40.360 --> 00:27:44.270
You have to create your own
frame, your own language,

00:27:44.270 --> 00:27:47.190
and you have to be
ruthless and brutal

00:27:47.190 --> 00:27:50.830
in pursuit of something good.

00:27:50.830 --> 00:27:54.160
The second strategy is
mobilizing popular support.

00:27:54.160 --> 00:27:56.828
We're seeing this at
school board level.

00:27:56.828 --> 00:27:59.370
After the reporting that I did
and some other colleagues have

00:27:59.370 --> 00:28:03.080
then picked up on about critical
race theory in public schools,

00:28:03.080 --> 00:28:09.660
I saw this amazing emergence,
organically, of irate parents

00:28:09.660 --> 00:28:12.630
coming home from work, tired,
in their dress clothes,

00:28:12.630 --> 00:28:14.730
showing up at those
school board meetings

00:28:14.730 --> 00:28:17.050
and giving hell to those
school board officials.

00:28:17.050 --> 00:28:18.910
Did you see any of these videos?

00:28:18.910 --> 00:28:20.930
Yeah.

00:28:20.930 --> 00:28:24.740
And we know for a fact that
these parents didn't know what

00:28:24.740 --> 00:28:26.810
was happening in the classroom.

00:28:26.810 --> 00:28:29.880
They never voted or
approved of this.

00:28:29.880 --> 00:28:33.330
And when they found out what
was happening, they were livid.

00:28:33.330 --> 00:28:36.270
And so parents are getting
mobilized on that grassroots

00:28:36.270 --> 00:28:40.970
level to oppose this kind
of ideological capture

00:28:40.970 --> 00:28:43.790
in those K-through-12
school districts.

00:28:43.790 --> 00:28:47.900
And when they won't listen,
which is much of the time,

00:28:47.900 --> 00:28:50.210
parents who have organized
and run campaigns

00:28:50.210 --> 00:28:52.700
are winning
overwhelming elections--

00:28:52.700 --> 00:28:54.770
we're talking 70-30.

00:28:54.770 --> 00:28:57.860
If you put your name on the
ballot in the median school

00:28:57.860 --> 00:28:59.930
district in the United
States and you say,

00:28:59.930 --> 00:29:02.110
I'm the anti-critical
race theory candidate,

00:29:02.110 --> 00:29:03.860
I'm going to get our
education system back

00:29:03.860 --> 00:29:08.090
to the basics of reading,
writing, math, et cetera,

00:29:08.090 --> 00:29:11.870
prioritizing excellence
over ideology,

00:29:11.870 --> 00:29:16.220
you're going to win, in many
places, at that 70-30 level,

00:29:16.220 --> 00:29:18.440
I think reflecting
overwhelming public support.

00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:27.790
And I think the third step,
then, is to decentralize.

00:29:27.790 --> 00:29:31.000
Because a lot of
these problems emerge

00:29:31.000 --> 00:29:36.160
because of centralization,
because of bureaucratic rule,

00:29:36.160 --> 00:29:39.550
because of control by a
minority of permanently

00:29:39.550 --> 00:29:42.310
publicly-subsidized
activists that

00:29:42.310 --> 00:29:46.390
have turned public institutions
into private ideological

00:29:46.390 --> 00:29:48.238
organizations.

00:29:48.238 --> 00:29:49.780
And I think, when
you look at schools

00:29:49.780 --> 00:29:51.260
as another good example--

00:29:51.260 --> 00:29:52.843
we can talk about
universities, maybe,

00:29:52.843 --> 00:29:54.250
if we have a little time--

00:29:54.250 --> 00:29:56.260
but when you talk
about schools, there's

00:29:56.260 --> 00:30:00.900
no reason that, if
you're a parent,

00:30:00.900 --> 00:30:02.400
you don't have the
means, let's say,

00:30:02.400 --> 00:30:05.520
to send your kid to private
school or parochial school,

00:30:05.520 --> 00:30:07.770
that you should be stuck
in a public school,

00:30:07.770 --> 00:30:11.240
based on your zip code,
that violates your child's

00:30:11.240 --> 00:30:14.630
right to conscience, that
shames or demoralizes

00:30:14.630 --> 00:30:19.200
your child on the basis
of race or identity,

00:30:19.200 --> 00:30:22.190
and that you're
allowed no recourse

00:30:22.190 --> 00:30:26.340
through the bureaucracy
and no escape mechanism.

00:30:26.340 --> 00:30:27.840
There's no reason
why we can't have,

00:30:27.840 --> 00:30:31.980
for example, universal
school choice

00:30:31.980 --> 00:30:35.190
so that a family that says, you
know what, this isn't for me;

00:30:35.190 --> 00:30:38.370
this violates our family's
values at a deep level,

00:30:38.370 --> 00:30:42.390
shouldn't be able to take
that average of $15,000 a year

00:30:42.390 --> 00:30:45.143
and move it wherever they want.

00:30:45.143 --> 00:30:46.560
It could be another
public school,

00:30:46.560 --> 00:30:50.230
it could be a charter school,
it could be a private school.

00:30:50.230 --> 00:30:51.980
This is controversial--
I don't know why--

00:30:51.980 --> 00:30:54.320
but it could also be
to a religious school.

00:30:54.320 --> 00:30:55.820
Because what's
happening is that you

00:30:55.820 --> 00:31:00.890
have 90% of American
schoolchildren attending

00:31:00.890 --> 00:31:05.150
public =, you have 90% of
American university students

00:31:05.150 --> 00:31:07.130
attending public universities.

00:31:07.130 --> 00:31:12.770
You have a monopoly that, unless
it's broken up, unless parents

00:31:12.770 --> 00:31:15.650
are given more power, more
freedom, more options,

00:31:15.650 --> 00:31:18.350
more choice, they're
going to be forced

00:31:18.350 --> 00:31:19.910
to run their kids
through a gauntlet

00:31:19.910 --> 00:31:20.910
that they can't control.

00:31:24.180 --> 00:31:27.650
And so I think if you
categorize them all together,

00:31:27.650 --> 00:31:30.530
to summarize very
briefly, you're

00:31:30.530 --> 00:31:33.200
engaging in a narrative war.

00:31:33.200 --> 00:31:38.300
I mentioned earlier that
there's this kind of postmodern

00:31:38.300 --> 00:31:44.030
layering on top of all of
our institutions to turn the,

00:31:44.030 --> 00:31:46.460
let's say, revolutionary
ideology into something

00:31:46.460 --> 00:31:51.350
very gauzy, very vague, very
seductive, very attractive,

00:31:51.350 --> 00:31:54.630
soft language.

00:31:54.630 --> 00:31:57.860
We have to rip that veil
off of what's happening

00:31:57.860 --> 00:32:01.638
and show Americans, through
reporting, through information,

00:32:01.638 --> 00:32:03.930
through investigation, through
public records requests,

00:32:03.930 --> 00:32:07.290
et cetera, exactly
what's happening.

00:32:07.290 --> 00:32:09.840
And we have to fight at that
narrative and symbolic level.

00:32:09.840 --> 00:32:13.170
We live in an
information society.

00:32:13.170 --> 00:32:16.170
And if we are unilaterally
outmanned on that front,

00:32:16.170 --> 00:32:18.507
it's impossible
to gain traction.

00:32:18.507 --> 00:32:20.340
Someone could go to the
school board meeting

00:32:20.340 --> 00:32:25.070
and literally not have the words
to oppose what's happening.

00:32:25.070 --> 00:32:26.570
And so that's what
I've tried to do,

00:32:26.570 --> 00:32:30.650
what others are now also
doing, is providing people

00:32:30.650 --> 00:32:36.530
a kind of key into explaining,
beyond the euphemism, exactly

00:32:36.530 --> 00:32:39.650
what's happening,
what those words mean,

00:32:39.650 --> 00:32:42.560
ripping the cover
off of that language

00:32:42.560 --> 00:32:46.220
and providing them with
a renewed moral language

00:32:46.220 --> 00:32:50.270
to be waging the fight,
not within the confines

00:32:50.270 --> 00:32:56.860
of the opposition's framing,
but by creating a new frame.

00:32:56.860 --> 00:32:58.570
And so with critical
race theory,

00:32:58.570 --> 00:33:02.680
for example, it creates
an entirely new frame.

00:33:02.680 --> 00:33:06.970
You're fighting substantively,
accurately, but also

00:33:06.970 --> 00:33:09.250
with language that
you can point to,

00:33:09.250 --> 00:33:12.010
you can go to a school board
meeting, you can explain,

00:33:12.010 --> 00:33:14.113
and you can oppose.

00:33:14.113 --> 00:33:16.530
And the evidence shows that,
from polling data that's been

00:33:16.530 --> 00:33:21.300
done sequentially over the
last 18 months, 18 months ago,

00:33:21.300 --> 00:33:23.640
let's say two years
ago-- round up--

00:33:23.640 --> 00:33:28.175
0% of the American people knew
about critical race theory.

00:33:28.175 --> 00:33:30.300
In the last 18 months,
we've brought that number up

00:33:30.300 --> 00:33:33.420
to 75% of the American people.

00:33:33.420 --> 00:33:37.060
And we've turned it into
a 2-to-1 issue, which,

00:33:37.060 --> 00:33:37.690
in politics--

00:33:37.690 --> 00:33:39.760
I don't know how many
of you are in politics--

00:33:39.760 --> 00:33:44.210
if you go to, let's say, a
campaign manager and you say,

00:33:44.210 --> 00:33:46.330
hey, we have a 2-to-1 issue--

00:33:46.330 --> 00:33:50.680
they're going to be salivating,
and drooling, and calling in ad

00:33:50.680 --> 00:33:53.140
buys immediately.

00:33:53.140 --> 00:33:57.095
That's a decisive victory in an
otherwise fairly evenly-divided

00:33:57.095 --> 00:33:57.595
society.

00:34:00.810 --> 00:34:04.580
The second is to attack the
credibility of institutions.

00:34:04.580 --> 00:34:07.430
This is something that I think
conservatives have been rightly

00:34:07.430 --> 00:34:09.440
hesitant to do for many years.

00:34:09.440 --> 00:34:12.739
But we like our institutions.

00:34:12.739 --> 00:34:15.260
I think conservatives
are really realizing now,

00:34:15.260 --> 00:34:17.840
we like what our
institutions were.

00:34:17.840 --> 00:34:20.540
We like what we
imagine them to be.

00:34:20.540 --> 00:34:23.580
And now that we are
seeing what they are,

00:34:23.580 --> 00:34:26.159
we don't like them as much.

00:34:26.159 --> 00:34:27.929
And so, luckily,
the institutions

00:34:27.929 --> 00:34:30.420
are really doing
this to themselves.

00:34:30.420 --> 00:34:35.040
You see trust in all of
our institutions cratering.

00:34:35.040 --> 00:34:36.670
I think it's foolhardy
to say, well,

00:34:36.670 --> 00:34:38.670
we really want to just
raise that number.

00:34:38.670 --> 00:34:40.679
I think you want to
create the conditions

00:34:40.679 --> 00:34:44.280
for fundamental,
structural change,

00:34:44.280 --> 00:34:47.010
to appropriate some language.

00:34:47.010 --> 00:34:49.350
For example, school choice--

00:34:49.350 --> 00:34:52.139
to get universal school
choice, you really

00:34:52.139 --> 00:34:54.910
need to operate from a premise
of universal public school

00:34:54.910 --> 00:34:55.410
distrust.

00:34:59.010 --> 00:35:03.030
Because in order for people
to take significant action,

00:35:03.030 --> 00:35:06.390
they have to feel like they
have something at stake.

00:35:06.390 --> 00:35:09.840
I think that the public schools
have done a remarkable job

00:35:09.840 --> 00:35:10.590
at doing that--

00:35:10.590 --> 00:35:13.320
specifically the public
school teachers unions.

00:35:13.320 --> 00:35:16.600
They shut down schools
for more than a year.

00:35:16.600 --> 00:35:18.940
In some districts,
half of all kids

00:35:18.940 --> 00:35:21.240
never showed up to
online learning.

00:35:21.240 --> 00:35:22.990
They've had the most
catastrophic learning

00:35:22.990 --> 00:35:27.530
loss likely since World War II.

00:35:27.530 --> 00:35:32.420
They kept kids masked for
years while the adults traveled

00:35:32.420 --> 00:35:35.300
around the world
with no mask on.

00:35:35.300 --> 00:35:38.480
And the teachers union endorsed
promoting critical race theory

00:35:38.480 --> 00:35:40.730
in all 14,000 local
school districts.

00:35:44.060 --> 00:35:47.570
And I think, finally, and
the positive side of this,

00:35:47.570 --> 00:35:49.340
is we have to
create alternatives.

00:35:51.910 --> 00:35:54.570
I think that in
a dynamic society

00:35:54.570 --> 00:35:57.420
like the society
that we have, we also

00:35:57.420 --> 00:36:00.610
have the opportunity
and the responsibility

00:36:00.610 --> 00:36:03.180
to create alternatives.

00:36:03.180 --> 00:36:10.160
And in many cases, I
think many of us have,

00:36:10.160 --> 00:36:11.930
and many parents, for example--

00:36:11.930 --> 00:36:17.330
I include myself in this
for a while, not anymore--

00:36:17.330 --> 00:36:19.100
got complacent.

00:36:19.100 --> 00:36:21.020
Well, I'll send
my kids to school.

00:36:21.020 --> 00:36:24.170
Should be fine, kind of
like what I had growing up.

00:36:24.170 --> 00:36:27.020
And I just don't think that
that's a realistic option

00:36:27.020 --> 00:36:28.470
for people anymore.

00:36:28.470 --> 00:36:31.910
And when I talk to parents, for
example, all over the country,

00:36:31.910 --> 00:36:36.590
they're desperate for
new ideas, new options.

00:36:36.590 --> 00:36:41.300
You have, now, 5 million
American kids in home school.

00:36:41.300 --> 00:36:44.060
There's just been an
explosion in homeschooling.

00:36:44.060 --> 00:36:45.800
You have even the
Hillsdale charter

00:36:45.800 --> 00:36:48.260
schools that are now expanding.

00:36:48.260 --> 00:36:52.520
Parents are desperate for
intellectual entrepreneurship

00:36:52.520 --> 00:36:55.240
in a sense, political
entrepreneurship,

00:36:55.240 --> 00:36:56.990
rather than just simply
fighting about who

00:36:56.990 --> 00:37:01.160
gets what, actually creating
something substantive that

00:37:01.160 --> 00:37:03.680
matches people's values.

00:37:03.680 --> 00:37:07.420
And so before we
take questions, I'd

00:37:07.420 --> 00:37:12.520
just like to close by
repeating the stakes,

00:37:12.520 --> 00:37:15.730
by resetting the stakes, rather.

00:37:15.730 --> 00:37:18.370
I really think that
the key dynamic for us

00:37:18.370 --> 00:37:21.310
to all be thinking
about is not necessarily

00:37:21.310 --> 00:37:24.870
even a left-versus-right
dynamic.

00:37:24.870 --> 00:37:25.830
It is that.

00:37:25.830 --> 00:37:26.790
That's important.

00:37:26.790 --> 00:37:29.160
But I think where
the opportunity

00:37:29.160 --> 00:37:33.210
lies is really a
top-and-bottom dynamic, so

00:37:33.210 --> 00:37:37.440
a kind of elite
class that represents

00:37:37.440 --> 00:37:41.670
a very small amount of people
that has positions of influence

00:37:41.670 --> 00:37:45.360
within the kind of
knowledge-making institutions,

00:37:45.360 --> 00:37:48.930
within pedagogical
institutions, that has designed,

00:37:48.930 --> 00:37:51.180
and implemented, and
distributed all of these ideas

00:37:51.180 --> 00:37:52.920
that we're finding
in the reporting,

00:37:52.920 --> 00:37:54.990
and the vast majority
of the American people

00:37:54.990 --> 00:37:57.210
who are still
attached to the idea

00:37:57.210 --> 00:38:00.440
that America is
a force for good,

00:38:00.440 --> 00:38:03.220
that their kids
should be protected

00:38:03.220 --> 00:38:07.600
from radical ideologies being
imposed on them at the tender

00:38:07.600 --> 00:38:11.650
ages of elementary
school, for example,

00:38:11.650 --> 00:38:17.090
and that our country, in some
ways, has gone off the rails.

00:38:17.090 --> 00:38:19.780
And they want a
sense of security.

00:38:19.780 --> 00:38:22.570
They want a sense that
people are fighting for them.

00:38:22.570 --> 00:38:27.010
And I really think there's
a common intuition of all

00:38:27.010 --> 00:38:32.410
of the good that
can still be raised

00:38:32.410 --> 00:38:35.410
into our institutions,
raised into our country,

00:38:35.410 --> 00:38:38.410
maybe not in the exact way that
it looks now, but in the way

00:38:38.410 --> 00:38:40.280
that it could be.

00:38:40.280 --> 00:38:44.420
And so I really think
it's up to all of us.

00:38:44.420 --> 00:38:47.780
You could be maybe on offense.

00:38:47.780 --> 00:38:52.430
There's some of us, I think me
included, that really are just

00:38:52.430 --> 00:38:54.830
built for offense.

00:38:54.830 --> 00:38:58.190
And there's some that are built
for creating institutions,

00:38:58.190 --> 00:39:01.250
for playing defense,
for protecting

00:39:01.250 --> 00:39:05.270
the civilizational
values and virtues that

00:39:05.270 --> 00:39:07.685
should be continued from
the past into the future.

00:39:12.030 --> 00:39:15.590
And I think that if we
are just taking these two

00:39:15.590 --> 00:39:21.740
poles, these two systems,
these two worldviews,

00:39:21.740 --> 00:39:27.680
we really have an option
between the revolution of 1776

00:39:27.680 --> 00:39:30.960
or the revolution of 1968.

00:39:30.960 --> 00:39:34.110
And I think one of
them offers a method

00:39:34.110 --> 00:39:39.900
for this incredible unfolding
of those founding principles

00:39:39.900 --> 00:39:42.840
of freedom and equality,
and the other one

00:39:42.840 --> 00:39:48.190
ends up in nihilism and
despair and demoralization.

00:39:48.190 --> 00:39:51.640
It ends up in a bombed-out
bunker in Greenwich Village

00:39:51.640 --> 00:39:54.170
in the 1970s.

00:39:54.170 --> 00:39:58.040
And so I would suggest
to you, even some of you

00:39:58.040 --> 00:40:02.957
who may not be
temperamentally predisposed,

00:40:02.957 --> 00:40:04.790
that the best strategy
in the immediate term

00:40:04.790 --> 00:40:07.162
is the siege of
the institutions,

00:40:07.162 --> 00:40:08.870
and that we should
prepare for the fight.

00:40:08.870 --> 00:40:09.920
Thank you so much.

00:40:09.920 --> 00:40:11.920
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:19.920 --> 00:40:20.640
Questions?

00:40:20.640 --> 00:40:22.140
LINNEA SHIVELY:
Thank you, Mr. Rufo.

00:40:22.140 --> 00:40:24.047
We will now have time
for some questions.

00:40:24.047 --> 00:40:26.130
If you have a brief question,
please make your way

00:40:26.130 --> 00:40:27.000
to a microphone.

00:40:29.748 --> 00:40:30.565
AUDIENCE: Awesome.

00:40:30.565 --> 00:40:32.190
Thank you so much
for your talk, Chris.

00:40:32.190 --> 00:40:32.640
CHRISTOPHER RUFO: Sure.

00:40:32.640 --> 00:40:34.290
AUDIENCE: I want to
touch on something

00:40:34.290 --> 00:40:37.500
that you said here in your
talk about narrative warfare.

00:40:37.500 --> 00:40:39.990
One of the biggest problems
I think we face right now

00:40:39.990 --> 00:40:43.030
is social media censorship.

00:40:43.030 --> 00:40:46.545
So you broke this story
about Disney on Twitter.

00:40:46.545 --> 00:40:48.420
And, obviously, you have
a lot of clout there

00:40:48.420 --> 00:40:50.370
and the story can
spread like wildfire.

00:40:50.370 --> 00:40:53.160
And it's obviously a
weapon that the left

00:40:53.160 --> 00:40:56.220
can use is to simply silence
those voices they don't like.

00:40:56.220 --> 00:40:59.490
And so in some ways, it
feels that the position

00:40:59.490 --> 00:41:01.950
in the narrative
war is very fragile.

00:41:01.950 --> 00:41:03.510
What are some of
the ways that you

00:41:03.510 --> 00:41:06.180
think we, as conservatives,
can get around

00:41:06.180 --> 00:41:09.695
the very obvious opposition
we face from big tech?

00:41:09.695 --> 00:41:11.070
One of the thoughts
that I've had

00:41:11.070 --> 00:41:15.630
is that attacks on the monopoly
status of many of these firms

00:41:15.630 --> 00:41:19.080
is absolutely
necessary to ensure

00:41:19.080 --> 00:41:21.597
that we have the platform to
be able to spread the message

00:41:21.597 --> 00:41:22.680
like critical race theory.

00:41:22.680 --> 00:41:23.040
Thank you.

00:41:23.040 --> 00:41:23.340
CHRISTOPHER RUFO: Great.

00:41:23.340 --> 00:41:23.840
Thanks.

00:41:23.840 --> 00:41:25.690
Great question.

00:41:25.690 --> 00:41:28.410
I think that the Disney
story is a perfect example

00:41:28.410 --> 00:41:31.520
of a potential answer, or at
least a suggestion of answer.

00:41:33.952 --> 00:41:35.410
One of the things
that I personally

00:41:35.410 --> 00:41:37.870
like about the
governor of Florida--

00:41:37.870 --> 00:41:40.210
and I've been able to
actually work with his team

00:41:40.210 --> 00:41:42.730
on policy on some
of these issues--

00:41:42.730 --> 00:41:48.760
is that he's not unwilling to
stand up to big companies, even

00:41:48.760 --> 00:41:50.500
the big companies in his state.

00:41:50.500 --> 00:41:54.460
I think he recognizes that our
corporations should ultimately

00:41:54.460 --> 00:41:58.090
be serving the common good,
should be serving the country,

00:41:58.090 --> 00:42:03.280
and he understands also that the
political power is ultimately

00:42:03.280 --> 00:42:05.590
higher than business power.

00:42:05.590 --> 00:42:10.180
And through the legislature, we
can set the rules of the game.

00:42:10.180 --> 00:42:12.850
If there's anti-censorship
legislation,

00:42:12.850 --> 00:42:15.430
I think there's some
commonsense regulation that

00:42:15.430 --> 00:42:17.530
could happen to protect people.

00:42:20.050 --> 00:42:24.790
Accepting that the digital
communications infrastructure

00:42:24.790 --> 00:42:29.560
is the new public square or
the digital public square.

00:42:29.560 --> 00:42:31.670
And I think you also
wield power, for example,

00:42:31.670 --> 00:42:33.370
against a company like Disney.

00:42:33.370 --> 00:42:38.020
If Disney is actively
undermining the families

00:42:38.020 --> 00:42:41.710
and voters of, say,
Florida, I know

00:42:41.710 --> 00:42:44.740
that I've been looking into
it, and Governor DeSantis

00:42:44.740 --> 00:42:47.440
has been looking into it,
and Congressman Jim Banks,

00:42:47.440 --> 00:42:51.370
for example, from nearby, in
Indiana, is looking into it--

00:42:51.370 --> 00:42:55.150
well, let's see if we can level
the playing field a little bit.

00:42:55.150 --> 00:42:58.330
Disney gets massive tax
breaks from Florida.

00:42:58.330 --> 00:43:01.840
Legislators are
looking into those.

00:43:01.840 --> 00:43:05.980
Congress has extended Disney's
copyright, essentially doing

00:43:05.980 --> 00:43:09.670
their bidding, in perpetuity,
multiple times longer

00:43:09.670 --> 00:43:11.450
than what the
founders had intended

00:43:11.450 --> 00:43:14.530
in their 28-year
copyright limit.

00:43:14.530 --> 00:43:17.440
And Republicans,
including Jim Banks,

00:43:17.440 --> 00:43:20.500
have said, clearly, when
Disney's copyrights on things

00:43:20.500 --> 00:43:22.810
like Mickey Mouse
expire next year

00:43:22.810 --> 00:43:24.640
and they come, hat
in hand, to Congress,

00:43:24.640 --> 00:43:26.980
saying, you better extend
it, you better extend it,

00:43:26.980 --> 00:43:29.270
conservatives are
going to say, no,

00:43:29.270 --> 00:43:30.500
we're not going to extend it.

00:43:30.500 --> 00:43:31.240
We're going to
allow those things

00:43:31.240 --> 00:43:32.840
to go into the public domain.

00:43:32.840 --> 00:43:36.760
And so I think you
can, in some ways,

00:43:36.760 --> 00:43:41.590
remind companies that the
deal that corporate executives

00:43:41.590 --> 00:43:44.080
thought that they had
in the last few years

00:43:44.080 --> 00:43:48.098
is we've already won
with conservatives.

00:43:48.098 --> 00:43:48.890
We bought them off.

00:43:48.890 --> 00:43:50.000
We have low tax rates.

00:43:50.000 --> 00:43:52.340
We have competitive
economic environment.

00:43:52.340 --> 00:43:54.020
It's better to be
a big corporation

00:43:54.020 --> 00:43:56.520
than a sole proprietor or a
small business in the United

00:43:56.520 --> 00:43:57.500
States.

00:43:57.500 --> 00:44:00.590
And so we've bought them
off on economic issues.

00:44:00.590 --> 00:44:01.700
We bought off the right.

00:44:01.700 --> 00:44:05.360
And so we're going to buy off
the left on culture issues.

00:44:05.360 --> 00:44:08.330
And they're finding out that
that relationship can shift

00:44:08.330 --> 00:44:09.990
and the power can change.

00:44:09.990 --> 00:44:14.720
And then you have wild
cards like Elon Musk.

00:44:14.720 --> 00:44:16.813
I don't know anything
about him personally.

00:44:16.813 --> 00:44:17.480
I never met him.

00:44:17.480 --> 00:44:24.810
But I think there's
just a feeling right now

00:44:24.810 --> 00:44:30.000
that we can tap into to maybe
channel that Teddy Roosevelt

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:32.910
spirit where we want to have
free enterprise, we want

00:44:32.910 --> 00:44:36.390
to have free competition, but
what we don't want to have is

00:44:36.390 --> 00:44:40.230
cartel organizations,
ideological and economic

00:44:40.230 --> 00:44:43.830
cartels, dictating the
terms from up high down

00:44:43.830 --> 00:44:46.400
to the average person.

00:44:46.400 --> 00:44:49.918
And so I'm optimistic
about all that.

00:44:49.918 --> 00:44:51.210
AUDIENCE: Thank you for coming.

00:44:51.210 --> 00:44:52.740
And I appreciate what you said.

00:44:52.740 --> 00:44:55.320
I can't wait to
revisit it online.

00:44:55.320 --> 00:44:58.320
But my wife and I
moved up here in 2013

00:44:58.320 --> 00:44:59.850
to put our kids in
Hillsdale Academy

00:44:59.850 --> 00:45:01.750
to get them out of the
public school system.

00:45:01.750 --> 00:45:02.820
So I appreciate that.

00:45:02.820 --> 00:45:04.680
We did what we
needed to do there.

00:45:04.680 --> 00:45:07.413
And we did cancel our
Disney account this--

00:45:07.413 --> 00:45:08.580
CHRISTOPHER RUFO: Well done.

00:45:08.580 --> 00:45:09.550
AUDIENCE: --past week.

00:45:09.550 --> 00:45:11.050
I've been needing
a reason to do it.

00:45:11.050 --> 00:45:11.940
It's been itching.

00:45:11.940 --> 00:45:13.890
But that's not what
I wanted to ask you.

00:45:13.890 --> 00:45:16.980
I despise the term,
racism, because I

00:45:16.980 --> 00:45:18.840
believe we're one race, right?

00:45:18.840 --> 00:45:21.930
There's different counts of
melanin in a person's skin,

00:45:21.930 --> 00:45:23.070
but we're one race.

00:45:23.070 --> 00:45:28.380
And I despise that term,
because it breeds division.

00:45:28.380 --> 00:45:30.330
And what would you
say-- how can we

00:45:30.330 --> 00:45:33.240
overcome that particular term?

00:45:33.240 --> 00:45:36.120
One of the beauties
of America's tapestry

00:45:36.120 --> 00:45:39.420
is that we come from so
many diverse ethnicities,

00:45:39.420 --> 00:45:43.500
and tribes, and nations, and
tongues, but we're one nation.

00:45:43.500 --> 00:45:46.085
And what would you suggest?

00:45:46.085 --> 00:45:47.460
CHRISTOPHER RUFO:
I would suggest

00:45:47.460 --> 00:45:52.950
that the vast majority of people
agree with you in the sense

00:45:52.950 --> 00:45:58.320
that we are one nation;
we come from all

00:45:58.320 --> 00:46:04.680
over the planet, really a
unique composition of people;

00:46:04.680 --> 00:46:07.260
and that we want to have
narratives, and especially

00:46:07.260 --> 00:46:14.940
pedagogies, that inspire kids,
that help kids transcend maybe

00:46:14.940 --> 00:46:18.690
their differences, aiming
them towards excellence,

00:46:18.690 --> 00:46:22.290
aiming them towards their
greatest accomplishments.

00:46:22.290 --> 00:46:27.750
And as you look at the public
polling data, it's very clear.

00:46:27.750 --> 00:46:30.660
The Manhattan Institute
released polling data

00:46:30.660 --> 00:46:32.665
from America's 20
fastest-growing cities.

00:46:32.665 --> 00:46:34.290
These are places that
are very diverse,

00:46:34.290 --> 00:46:36.115
there's a lot of
young families, people

00:46:36.115 --> 00:46:37.740
trying to move their
way up the ladder,

00:46:37.740 --> 00:46:39.940
live the American dream.

00:46:39.940 --> 00:46:43.000
And the numbers were
quite clear among parents.

00:46:43.000 --> 00:46:46.240
Parents opposed lessons
on critical race theory,

00:46:46.240 --> 00:46:49.810
systemic racism, and white
privilege by, I believe,

00:46:49.810 --> 00:46:52.660
a 42% margin--

00:46:52.660 --> 00:46:53.830
overwhelming.

00:46:53.830 --> 00:46:58.220
And this includes a majority
of parents who are white,

00:46:58.220 --> 00:47:01.540
Black, Latino, Asian--

00:47:01.540 --> 00:47:05.640
so a majority of every
racial demographic.

00:47:05.640 --> 00:47:09.240
And they want to have
an education that

00:47:09.240 --> 00:47:14.470
speaks to them the values of
all of these everyday people.

00:47:14.470 --> 00:47:18.030
They said, look, I just want
to have my kid have a chance

00:47:18.030 --> 00:47:19.770
to come up in the
world, to do better

00:47:19.770 --> 00:47:24.270
than I could do, to get a great
job, to raise a great family.

00:47:24.270 --> 00:47:29.220
And these are kind of the humble
but universal building blocks

00:47:29.220 --> 00:47:31.290
that I see people
tapping into every day.

00:47:31.290 --> 00:47:34.170
And they sense that
it's going to be crushed

00:47:34.170 --> 00:47:38.250
by these people who occupy
positions of enormous power

00:47:38.250 --> 00:47:45.790
and privilege that simply have
this poisonous and nihilistic

00:47:45.790 --> 00:47:48.640
ideology--

00:47:48.640 --> 00:47:51.400
wherever it comes from,
whatever its lineage is--

00:47:51.400 --> 00:47:54.335
that they want to impose
from the top down.

00:47:54.335 --> 00:47:55.960
And so I think the
best way to overcome

00:47:55.960 --> 00:47:57.653
it is not to be a Pollyanna.

00:47:57.653 --> 00:48:00.070
You have to acknowledge the
historical existence of racism

00:48:00.070 --> 00:48:02.200
in the United
States, for example,

00:48:02.200 --> 00:48:04.900
but always put it in the
context of the country's highest

00:48:04.900 --> 00:48:07.630
ideals and the country's
steady progress

00:48:07.630 --> 00:48:09.520
towards realizing them.

00:48:09.520 --> 00:48:11.350
And I think it's a
unifying message.

00:48:11.350 --> 00:48:12.850
And wherever I go,
people I talk to,

00:48:12.850 --> 00:48:16.630
from all different
backgrounds, as long

00:48:16.630 --> 00:48:21.580
as you're not at the New York
Times building or something,

00:48:21.580 --> 00:48:23.470
it's something that
they want and they need.

00:48:23.470 --> 00:48:25.480
And we have to be
unafraid to talk about it.

00:48:28.510 --> 00:48:30.410
AUDIENCE: Chris, thank
you for your thoughts.

00:48:30.410 --> 00:48:31.785
One of the things
that bothers me

00:48:31.785 --> 00:48:34.000
is the total chaos
in the country.

00:48:34.000 --> 00:48:37.930
I wouldn't trust the FDA,
the CDC, for sure the FBI

00:48:37.930 --> 00:48:41.830
is equivalent to the
Gestapo, and maybe the CIA.

00:48:41.830 --> 00:48:44.620
The military, I don't know
if they're for fighting a war

00:48:44.620 --> 00:48:47.730
or fighting gender wars.

00:48:47.730 --> 00:48:51.790
What do you think about
what's going on in society

00:48:51.790 --> 00:48:53.470
beyond critical race theory?

00:48:53.470 --> 00:48:56.350
It seems to be a total
meltdown of trust

00:48:56.350 --> 00:48:59.320
in any of the institutions
on any of the issues,

00:48:59.320 --> 00:49:02.082
be they science or whatever.

00:49:02.082 --> 00:49:03.040
CHRISTOPHER RUFO: Yeah.

00:49:03.040 --> 00:49:04.730
I think the answer
is pretty simple.

00:49:04.730 --> 00:49:07.150
There's been a rupture
in trust because a lot

00:49:07.150 --> 00:49:10.050
of the institutions
have been untrustworthy.

00:49:10.050 --> 00:49:11.320
That's the simple fact.

00:49:11.320 --> 00:49:15.780
I don't think that trust
numbers have plummeted

00:49:15.780 --> 00:49:19.170
because our institutions have
been so wise, and virtuous,

00:49:19.170 --> 00:49:23.070
and fantastic, and we're just
the ungrateful kind of plebs

00:49:23.070 --> 00:49:25.740
below.

00:49:25.740 --> 00:49:26.340
Look at COVID.

00:49:29.770 --> 00:49:32.740
In a lot of cases-- this is
not my area of expertise--

00:49:32.740 --> 00:49:37.930
but from just a
personal point of view,

00:49:37.930 --> 00:49:40.960
all of these things that
were conspiracy theories,

00:49:40.960 --> 00:49:43.630
so-called conspiracy
theories by "The Science,"

00:49:43.630 --> 00:49:48.220
in capital letters,
turned out to be true,

00:49:48.220 --> 00:49:50.740
whether it's the lab leak,
or whether it's, well,

00:49:50.740 --> 00:49:52.810
you can't get COVID if
you have the vaccine,

00:49:52.810 --> 00:49:55.420
or you can't pass it along,
or we need to have endless

00:49:55.420 --> 00:50:00.010
masking, it's the
most effective plan,

00:50:00.010 --> 00:50:03.520
you look at the politics that
has been embedded in all these

00:50:03.520 --> 00:50:07.680
institutions, and then you
look at the decisions--

00:50:07.680 --> 00:50:11.550
I think I saw the most
heartbreaking numbers,

00:50:11.550 --> 00:50:15.240
for example, of businesses
that collapsed in the COVID

00:50:15.240 --> 00:50:16.710
lockdowns.

00:50:16.710 --> 00:50:21.090
Those are people's life
savings, their livelihood,

00:50:21.090 --> 00:50:24.740
their whole dream, the whole
thing they've worked towards.

00:50:24.740 --> 00:50:28.220
You have teenagers that lost
out on their high school

00:50:28.220 --> 00:50:29.390
experience.

00:50:29.390 --> 00:50:32.780
You have skyrocketing rates
of depression and suicide

00:50:32.780 --> 00:50:33.425
among kids.

00:50:36.120 --> 00:50:39.510
And then you turn around, as
parents are trying to say,

00:50:39.510 --> 00:50:41.670
I'm trying to keep
a job, I'm trying

00:50:41.670 --> 00:50:43.680
to keep my business
afloat, I'm trying

00:50:43.680 --> 00:50:48.300
to make sure my kid doesn't
sink into the abyss,

00:50:48.300 --> 00:50:51.390
I'm being lectured
on television,

00:50:51.390 --> 00:50:56.880
and then I drop into my kids'
third-grade social studies

00:50:56.880 --> 00:51:01.040
class, and he's being lectured
on critical race theory.

00:51:01.040 --> 00:51:03.620
It's a very simple response to
people's everyday experience,

00:51:03.620 --> 00:51:04.340
I think.

00:51:04.340 --> 00:51:05.930
And it's too bad.

00:51:05.930 --> 00:51:06.515
It's awful.

00:51:10.640 --> 00:51:12.800
I've kind of learned
this maybe the hard way,

00:51:12.800 --> 00:51:17.060
but these problems
aren't going to be solved

00:51:17.060 --> 00:51:19.880
by asking, very
nicely and politely,

00:51:19.880 --> 00:51:22.070
for the people in
positions of power

00:51:22.070 --> 00:51:23.615
to pretty please
do a better job.

00:51:26.345 --> 00:51:28.470
And you know what else is
something true, related--

00:51:28.470 --> 00:51:30.252
[APPLAUSE]

00:51:34.250 --> 00:51:36.690
I'll share an
experience from Disney.

00:51:36.690 --> 00:51:39.250
The other thing that
we need to stop doing

00:51:39.250 --> 00:51:45.700
is limiting our arguments
to things that are

00:51:45.700 --> 00:51:50.980
deemed appropriate by liberals.

00:51:50.980 --> 00:51:53.650
In journalism, you
wouldn't believe the kind

00:51:53.650 --> 00:51:55.960
of self-censorship--

00:51:55.960 --> 00:51:58.240
the biggest crisis
is self-censorship.

00:51:58.240 --> 00:52:01.570
It's not even getting
banned from social media.

00:52:01.570 --> 00:52:03.640
In the sense that, well--

00:52:03.640 --> 00:52:05.740
I wrote the story
about, hey, what

00:52:05.740 --> 00:52:10.860
is Disney's actual record
on child sex crimes?

00:52:10.860 --> 00:52:12.790
Let's be honest about it.

00:52:12.790 --> 00:52:15.300
You can't say that.

00:52:15.300 --> 00:52:17.400
Oh, that's just
absolutely horrible.

00:52:17.400 --> 00:52:20.500
That's out of bounds.

00:52:20.500 --> 00:52:23.520
No, it's not.

00:52:23.520 --> 00:52:27.740
And I think if we
internalize-- and it

00:52:27.740 --> 00:52:30.870
depends on how online
you are, but there's

00:52:30.870 --> 00:52:35.190
a kind of a classical liberal,
or a centrist liberal,

00:52:35.190 --> 00:52:39.230
or a kind of Bill Maher type.

00:52:39.230 --> 00:52:45.030
Appealing to people in that
category will get you nowhere.

00:52:45.030 --> 00:52:48.810
And do you want to try to get
the plaudits or the approval

00:52:48.810 --> 00:52:53.880
of people in that circle, in
the kind of left liberal media

00:52:53.880 --> 00:52:56.610
environment and follow their
kind of rules of discourse,

00:52:56.610 --> 00:52:59.010
and debate, and language?

00:52:59.010 --> 00:53:03.510
Or do you want to actually
align with the 60% of people

00:53:03.510 --> 00:53:05.820
who are fighting
for their companies,

00:53:05.820 --> 00:53:09.540
fighting for their jobs,
fighting for their kids, that

00:53:09.540 --> 00:53:11.905
are represented by
legislators at the state level

00:53:11.905 --> 00:53:13.530
and the federal level
that are actually

00:53:13.530 --> 00:53:15.190
going to get the job done?

00:53:15.190 --> 00:53:19.180
And too many people in my world,
in my field of journalism,

00:53:19.180 --> 00:53:20.710
they choose the former.

00:53:20.710 --> 00:53:23.748
And I like to think that,
in 15, 20, 30 years,

00:53:23.748 --> 00:53:25.540
they're going to realize,
wow, I was really

00:53:25.540 --> 00:53:29.570
polite this whole time but
I didn't achieve anything.

00:53:29.570 --> 00:53:32.020
And so I'd rather be impolite.

00:53:32.020 --> 00:53:34.960
I'd rather get in a
little bit of trouble.

00:53:34.960 --> 00:53:42.080
I'd rather make some of the
prominent people a little angry

00:53:42.080 --> 00:53:44.337
and actually deliver results.

00:53:44.337 --> 00:53:46.170
And so I think we're
doing that with Disney.

00:53:46.170 --> 00:53:49.270
I think the story, for
example, with these videos,

00:53:49.270 --> 00:53:53.350
it generated, at a bare
minimum, 100, 150 million media

00:53:53.350 --> 00:53:56.530
impressions.

00:53:56.530 --> 00:53:58.960
I think that it's
almost certain now

00:53:58.960 --> 00:54:00.940
that Republicans in
Congress will not

00:54:00.940 --> 00:54:03.760
extend their copyrights
next year when they expire.

00:54:03.760 --> 00:54:06.100
That's a multibillion
dollar hit to Disney.

00:54:06.100 --> 00:54:07.980
[APPLAUSE]

00:54:11.900 --> 00:54:14.400
And you're not going to get
that done by being really polite

00:54:14.400 --> 00:54:15.567
and following all the rules.

00:54:15.567 --> 00:54:16.380
I hate to say it.

00:54:16.380 --> 00:54:19.470
Sorry, kids.

00:54:19.470 --> 00:54:22.380
Be polite, at Hillsdale,
to your teachers

00:54:22.380 --> 00:54:23.400
and your administrators.

00:54:23.400 --> 00:54:27.940
But out there, toughen up.

00:54:27.940 --> 00:54:28.811
Right here.

00:54:28.811 --> 00:54:31.520
AUDIENCE: How much
influence-- [? or ?]

00:54:31.520 --> 00:54:38.360
mainly the 1619 Project that's
been an influence on CRT?

00:54:38.360 --> 00:54:40.910
CHRISTOPHER RUFO: OK,
question, 1619 Project.

00:54:40.910 --> 00:54:43.850
I've said this a
number of times--

00:54:43.850 --> 00:54:47.540
the 1619 Project is
an epiphenomenon.

00:54:47.540 --> 00:54:51.650
It is an ephemeral
creation that people

00:54:51.650 --> 00:54:54.890
are going to forget about
in, probably, a relatively

00:54:54.890 --> 00:54:56.738
short to medium period of time.

00:54:56.738 --> 00:54:58.280
I don't think it
makes a lot of sense

00:54:58.280 --> 00:55:00.470
to get too worked up about it.

00:55:00.470 --> 00:55:04.040
I think that it can
displace a lot of attention

00:55:04.040 --> 00:55:09.240
from the deeper issues about the
ideology, where it comes from,

00:55:09.240 --> 00:55:11.240
how it works bureaucratically.

00:55:11.240 --> 00:55:16.408
And I'm personally not too
worried about the 1619 Project.

00:55:16.408 --> 00:55:17.450
I'm not saying it's good.

00:55:17.450 --> 00:55:19.130
I don't like it.

00:55:19.130 --> 00:55:24.350
But in the sense that I don't
think that the 1619 Project is

00:55:24.350 --> 00:55:27.800
going to do grave
damage, I think

00:55:27.800 --> 00:55:34.070
we have to really keep our eye
focused at a much deeper level.

00:55:34.070 --> 00:55:38.090
And the question is, how do
you blow up the transmission

00:55:38.090 --> 00:55:41.990
belt that delivers the
1619 Project to millions

00:55:41.990 --> 00:55:43.790
of school kids?

00:55:43.790 --> 00:55:46.510
And if you blow up
the transmission belt,

00:55:46.510 --> 00:55:49.010
the New York Times is going to
be stuck with a lot of copies

00:55:49.010 --> 00:55:51.965
of The 1619 Project.

00:55:51.965 --> 00:55:54.090
LINNEA SHIVELY: We have
time for one more question.

00:55:54.090 --> 00:55:55.950
CHRISTOPHER RUFO:
All right, great.

00:55:55.950 --> 00:55:58.920
AUDIENCE: This question is about
blowing up that transmission

00:55:58.920 --> 00:56:02.760
belt. Thank you for all you
do for opening our eyes.

00:56:02.760 --> 00:56:05.730
CASEL, the social-emotional
learning company

00:56:05.730 --> 00:56:07.950
that K-through-12
schools look to

00:56:07.950 --> 00:56:10.080
for their
social-emotional learning,

00:56:10.080 --> 00:56:15.030
they are now promoting equity
with excellence in education.

00:56:17.920 --> 00:56:24.070
How do we crank out
more non-woke teachers

00:56:24.070 --> 00:56:29.620
from our woke
universities and still

00:56:29.620 --> 00:56:33.760
be able to label them
as excellence when

00:56:33.760 --> 00:56:38.530
the academic world says, oh,
our public universities are

00:56:38.530 --> 00:56:43.850
excellent and we have
to have certified?

00:56:43.850 --> 00:56:45.440
Do we have a way to do that?

00:56:45.440 --> 00:56:46.815
CHRISTOPHER RUFO:
Great question.

00:56:46.815 --> 00:56:47.890
And we do.

00:56:47.890 --> 00:56:49.240
There's a couple of ways.

00:56:49.240 --> 00:56:52.900
I'll talk about maybe
the most effective way.

00:56:52.900 --> 00:56:57.010
Look, I know Michigan is kind of
a purple state-- purple state,

00:56:57.010 --> 00:56:58.480
would you consider it?

00:56:58.480 --> 00:57:00.340
Yeah, OK.

00:57:00.340 --> 00:57:01.840
But even red states--

00:57:01.840 --> 00:57:04.427
I talk to people in
red states, how do we

00:57:04.427 --> 00:57:06.760
deal with this horrible problem
of our public university

00:57:06.760 --> 00:57:07.660
system?

00:57:07.660 --> 00:57:08.320
I don't know.

00:57:08.320 --> 00:57:09.760
You're the state legislature.

00:57:09.760 --> 00:57:11.350
You pay for it.

00:57:11.350 --> 00:57:14.320
You pass appropriations
bills for it.

00:57:14.320 --> 00:57:16.540
It's under your jurisdiction.

00:57:16.540 --> 00:57:19.540
And every year, you
just write a blank check

00:57:19.540 --> 00:57:21.610
to the public university system.

00:57:21.610 --> 00:57:23.800
These are public
universities that

00:57:23.800 --> 00:57:27.280
should reflect and transmit
the values of the public

00:57:27.280 --> 00:57:29.560
and the representatives
of the public, i.e.

00:57:29.560 --> 00:57:33.580
state legislators, have ultimate
power to shape or reshape

00:57:33.580 --> 00:57:35.240
those institutions.

00:57:35.240 --> 00:57:37.270
And so we have to
get out of this idea

00:57:37.270 --> 00:57:40.750
that somehow a public
university system is

00:57:40.750 --> 00:57:44.510
a totally independent
entity that

00:57:44.510 --> 00:57:46.910
practices academic freedom--

00:57:46.910 --> 00:57:47.660
a total fraud.

00:57:47.660 --> 00:57:52.820
That's just a false statement--
fundamentally false--

00:57:52.820 --> 00:57:55.070
and that you can't
touch it or else you're

00:57:55.070 --> 00:57:59.810
impinging on the rights of
the gender studies department

00:57:59.810 --> 00:58:02.960
to follow their
dreams, you know?

00:58:02.960 --> 00:58:04.002
It really is that simple.

00:58:04.002 --> 00:58:05.960
And legislators you talk
to, they say, oh yeah,

00:58:05.960 --> 00:58:07.100
I didn't think about that.

00:58:07.100 --> 00:58:09.950
Because it's such in the
background of our experience.

00:58:09.950 --> 00:58:13.110
We accept it as a given.

00:58:13.110 --> 00:58:16.847
We accept it as the status quo
that we can't even examine.

00:58:16.847 --> 00:58:18.930
But I think legislators
are starting-- in Florida,

00:58:18.930 --> 00:58:19.410
they're doing it.

00:58:19.410 --> 00:58:20.710
In Texas, they're doing it.

00:58:20.710 --> 00:58:23.440
They're starting to
say, hey, wait a minute,

00:58:23.440 --> 00:58:29.620
why is every taxi driver,
short order cook, and plumber

00:58:29.620 --> 00:58:35.280
in my state paying six-figure
salaries for a diversity

00:58:35.280 --> 00:58:38.550
and inclusion department at
a public university where

00:58:38.550 --> 00:58:41.467
these folks are making
$300,000 or $400,000 a year,

00:58:41.467 --> 00:58:43.050
and you look at their
job description,

00:58:43.050 --> 00:58:45.360
it's indiscernible what they do.

00:58:48.770 --> 00:58:52.670
You're funding private political
activism with public dollars.

00:58:52.670 --> 00:58:55.580
So states can say, no more
public dollars for that.

00:58:55.580 --> 00:58:57.110
You can take it away.

00:58:57.110 --> 00:59:01.190
I think another thing that
states can do and should do

00:59:01.190 --> 00:59:03.680
is saying, well, we're
going to actually open

00:59:03.680 --> 00:59:06.350
a new branch of our
public university system--

00:59:06.350 --> 00:59:09.450
say, the flagship University
of Michigan, Ann Arbor--

00:59:09.450 --> 00:59:11.200
conservatives, let's
say, have both houses

00:59:11.200 --> 00:59:13.480
and the governorship in
Michigan, for example--

00:59:13.480 --> 00:59:14.920
a policy you could pursue.

00:59:14.920 --> 00:59:17.230
We could say, we're going
to have an independent board

00:59:17.230 --> 00:59:19.930
of directors, appointed
by the governor,

00:59:19.930 --> 00:59:24.550
and we're going to have a
conservative center that

00:59:24.550 --> 00:59:27.670
is independently governed within
the University of Michigan

00:59:27.670 --> 00:59:29.530
or the University of Texas.

00:59:29.530 --> 00:59:33.190
And then you create a magnet
for conservative professors,

00:59:33.190 --> 00:59:36.070
you create a bit of debate
within the university,

00:59:36.070 --> 00:59:37.870
you create a variety
of perspectives,

00:59:37.870 --> 00:59:40.270
you create a
diversity of courses,

00:59:40.270 --> 00:59:44.707
and you're creating a
separate patronage system.

00:59:44.707 --> 00:59:46.790
Some people don't like
thinking about it that way.

00:59:46.790 --> 00:59:47.990
But guess what?

00:59:47.990 --> 00:59:50.990
The public universities, the DEI
departments, the public school

00:59:50.990 --> 00:59:52.850
bureaucracies are, at
the end of the day,

00:59:52.850 --> 00:59:58.330
patronage systems for
left-wing activists.

00:59:58.330 --> 01:00:01.000
And as long as there's going
to be a patronage system,

01:00:01.000 --> 01:00:03.880
wouldn't it be good to have
some people who are representing

01:00:03.880 --> 01:00:05.800
the public within them?

01:00:05.800 --> 01:00:08.410
I think the answer
is probably yes.

01:00:08.410 --> 01:00:13.510
And for teachers specifically,
there's absolutely no reason

01:00:13.510 --> 01:00:16.330
that teachers need
a master's degree

01:00:16.330 --> 01:00:19.520
to teach reading
to third graders.

01:00:19.520 --> 01:00:22.700
And in fact, all of the
academic studies, which

01:00:22.700 --> 01:00:26.180
are by no means right-leaning
or conservative,

01:00:26.180 --> 01:00:28.670
they show that
teacher credentialing

01:00:28.670 --> 01:00:31.490
and teachers'
master's programs have

01:00:31.490 --> 01:00:36.390
zero relationship with quality
teaching in the classroom.

01:00:36.390 --> 01:00:40.200
And in fact, many people,
education scholars

01:00:40.200 --> 01:00:42.630
whom I trust, say
that it actually

01:00:42.630 --> 01:00:47.580
does significant damage to
the public school workforce,

01:00:47.580 --> 01:00:51.320
because you have the
craziest people in charge

01:00:51.320 --> 01:00:52.910
of departments of education--

01:00:52.910 --> 01:00:54.800
I mean, really, truly.

01:00:54.800 --> 01:00:57.440
The number one
most-assigned textbook

01:00:57.440 --> 01:01:02.150
for graduate-level
departments of education

01:01:02.150 --> 01:01:06.440
is a book called Pedagogy of
the Oppressed by Paulo Freire.

01:01:06.440 --> 01:01:10.310
He was a Brazilian
Marxist-Leninist whose greatest

01:01:10.310 --> 01:01:14.180
claim to fame was running
the educational propaganda

01:01:14.180 --> 01:01:18.410
for the Marxist-Leninist
governments in West Africa

01:01:18.410 --> 01:01:20.330
after decolonization.

01:01:20.330 --> 01:01:22.850
And he very famously ran
the education program

01:01:22.850 --> 01:01:26.680
for Guinea-Bissau, the
West African nation.

01:01:26.680 --> 01:01:29.290
And some scholars went
back, years later,

01:01:29.290 --> 01:01:31.427
and he was heralded as
this genius of education,

01:01:31.427 --> 01:01:33.010
bringing education
to the countryside,

01:01:33.010 --> 01:01:35.343
educating the peasants, Paulo
Freire's teaching everyone

01:01:35.343 --> 01:01:36.250
to read.

01:01:36.250 --> 01:01:38.470
They actually
studied the results.

01:01:38.470 --> 01:01:41.020
They couldn't find
a single example

01:01:41.020 --> 01:01:44.680
of a single person that
learned basic literacy

01:01:44.680 --> 01:01:47.850
under his programs.

01:01:47.850 --> 01:01:48.600
And guess what?

01:01:48.600 --> 01:01:50.730
American public school
students are doing better

01:01:50.730 --> 01:01:53.280
than the public school students
in Guinea-Bissau, for sure.

01:01:53.280 --> 01:01:54.697
But you still have
about a quarter

01:01:54.697 --> 01:01:58.920
of them never achieve basic
literacy, despite education

01:01:58.920 --> 01:02:02.940
spending doubling or more in
the last generation or two

01:02:02.940 --> 01:02:04.660
generations.

01:02:04.660 --> 01:02:06.750
And so the simple
answer is, say,

01:02:06.750 --> 01:02:09.400
from the state level,
pass legislation--

01:02:09.400 --> 01:02:11.160
there is no financial
benefit and there's

01:02:11.160 --> 01:02:13.350
no requirement for
teachers to get

01:02:13.350 --> 01:02:15.930
advanced educational
credentials, degrees,

01:02:15.930 --> 01:02:17.313
master's, et cetera.

01:02:17.313 --> 01:02:19.230
And you can have someone
who's, say, a history

01:02:19.230 --> 01:02:23.010
major at Hillsdale College
that is excited, wants

01:02:23.010 --> 01:02:25.410
to learn how to teach,
can get a job teaching

01:02:25.410 --> 01:02:27.223
history at a public school.

01:02:27.223 --> 01:02:28.890
There's no reason
that you couldn't have

01:02:28.890 --> 01:02:30.250
a system as simple as that.

01:02:30.250 --> 01:02:35.460
And what that does is it cuts
out this cartel, essentially.

01:02:35.460 --> 01:02:38.010
You have to pass through the
"graduate school of education"

01:02:38.010 --> 01:02:38.657
cartel.

01:02:38.657 --> 01:02:40.740
If you want your piece of
paper in order to teach,

01:02:40.740 --> 01:02:42.100
you have to go
through their thing.

01:02:42.100 --> 01:02:43.475
So they have a
captured customer,

01:02:43.475 --> 01:02:45.850
a captured line of business.

01:02:45.850 --> 01:02:47.530
Get rid of it.

01:02:47.530 --> 01:02:49.180
Say, no more, statewide.

01:02:49.180 --> 01:02:52.330
If you want to go, you can
go-- spend your own money.

01:02:52.330 --> 01:02:53.290
It's not required.

01:02:53.290 --> 01:02:54.100
And guess what?

01:02:54.100 --> 01:02:57.640
I think nobody would go.

01:02:57.640 --> 01:03:00.260
Maybe some lunatics would go.

01:03:00.260 --> 01:03:01.150
And that's fine.

01:03:01.150 --> 01:03:03.160
But then you look
at them and say,

01:03:03.160 --> 01:03:05.650
don't hire the ones with
the master's, because those

01:03:05.650 --> 01:03:06.820
are the crazies.

01:03:06.820 --> 01:03:10.930
So I think a lot of it is, look,
public policy is the greatest

01:03:10.930 --> 01:03:12.280
tool that we have.

01:03:12.280 --> 01:03:15.730
Almost all of our problems
are created by public policy.

01:03:15.730 --> 01:03:18.910
Almost all of the worst
ideologues in our society

01:03:18.910 --> 01:03:21.790
are permanently subsidized
by public dollars.

01:03:21.790 --> 01:03:24.370
And so we need to have the
courage, and the intelligence,

01:03:24.370 --> 01:03:27.640
and the tenacity to say
what the public giveth

01:03:27.640 --> 01:03:29.940
the public can taketh away.

01:03:29.940 --> 01:03:32.900
And so we get in there, we
defund things we don't like,

01:03:32.900 --> 01:03:35.240
we fund things we
do like, and we

01:03:35.240 --> 01:03:38.420
try to break up the system
so that, ultimately, we're

01:03:38.420 --> 01:03:41.000
taking power away from those
centralized authorities

01:03:41.000 --> 01:03:42.980
and giving it back
to everyday people.

01:03:42.980 --> 01:03:48.140
Because I spend a lot of time
at both parts of our society,

01:03:48.140 --> 01:03:51.860
in the kind of fancy parts and
in the kind of everyday parts,

01:03:51.860 --> 01:03:55.750
and at the end of the
day, I would trust people

01:03:55.750 --> 01:03:59.470
in the middle, in
that big bottom,

01:03:59.470 --> 01:04:03.050
making sane and rational
and wise decisions.

01:04:03.050 --> 01:04:05.592
And so the more we can put
power into their hands,

01:04:05.592 --> 01:04:06.800
the better we're going to be.

01:04:06.800 --> 01:04:07.750
So I appreciate it.

01:04:07.750 --> 01:04:09.535
Thanks, everyone,
for coming out.

01:04:09.535 --> 01:04:11.515
[APPLAUSE]

01:04:24.308 --> 01:04:25.600
SPEAKER 1: Thanks again, Chris.

01:04:25.600 --> 01:04:27.430
For our guests, we
have a hospitality

01:04:27.430 --> 01:04:29.410
just out here in the lobby.

01:04:29.410 --> 01:04:32.830
If you leave hungry, it's
your own fault. For students,

01:04:32.830 --> 01:04:34.120
go do your homework.

01:04:34.120 --> 01:04:36.570
[LAUGHTER]

